# Prompt
You are the specification coordinator for the epam-cli orchestration system. Before every phase you read orchestrations/prd.json (implementationOrder and stories), evaluate risk, dependencies, and technical readiness, then assign the correct specification runners: OpenSpec, Speckit, both, or none. You write assignments into each story's specification.assignedAgents field along with coordinatorNotes, target provider/model, and timestamps. You understand the provider policy ledger (configuration.aiRuntime.providerPolicies), token guardrails, and know when to skip stories that were already hand-curated. When you plan a run you emit JSON compliant with spec-mode-runner.js so that automation can apply deltas deterministically. You never invent story IDs or mutate acceptance criteria directly; only delegate to spec agents and record their status.

You are the EPAM CLI specification coordinator reviewing the completed spec outputs for phase core.

Each story was processed by a sequential agent pipeline:
  1. openspec elaborated requirements (AC refinement, story splits, technical depth)
  2. speckit reviewed openspec's output (testability, security, edge cases, gap analysis)

For each story, evaluate the quality of the collaborative spec work:
1. Did both agents add meaningful, non-overlapping value?
2. Flag any story needing human review.

(Brownfield tickets never split and their acceptance criteria are immutable —
do not evaluate split quality or AC completeness; there is nothing there for
either agent to have changed.)

Respond with JSON between <SPEC_REVIEW> and </SPEC_REVIEW> using this schema:
[
  {"storyId":"REM-xxx","verdict":"approved|needs_review","reviewNotes":"coordinator observations","qualityScore":0.0-1.0,"flags":[]}
]

Stories to review:
[
  {
    "id": "MOCK-HW-1",
    "title": "Hello world greeting should say hello dolly",
    "acceptanceCriteria": [],
    "specification": {
      "runId": "20260803T150729Z",
      "assignedAgents": [
        "openspec"
      ],
      "coordinatorNotes": "Trivial hello-world story with empty acceptance criteria; assign openspec to elaborate and define ACs before implementation. Speckit not needed — no security, edge-case, or testability concerns for a simple greeting.",
      "status": "completed",
      "updatedAt": "2026-08-03T15:13:44.407Z",
      "appliedAgents": [
        "openspec"
      ],
      "agentContributions": [
        {
          "agent": "openspec",
          "applied": true,
          "notes": "Existing getGreeting() returns 'hello world'; ticket says it should return 'hello dolly'. This is a defect — the function computes the wrong value. Fix site is src/hello.ts / getGreeting. Acceptance criteria array is empty, so verification criteria are derived from the description alone.",
          "acceptanceChanged": false,
          "splitCount": 0,
          "timestamp": "2026-08-03T15:13:44.407Z"
        }
      ]
    }
  }
]

<SPEC_REVIEW>
</SPEC_REVIEW>

# Output
<SPEC_REVIEW>
[
  {
    "storyId": "MOCK-HW-1",
    "verdict": "needs_review",
    "reviewNotes": "Only openspec was assigned (speckit deliberately skipped for trivial story — acceptable coordinator decision). OpenSpec correctly diagnosed the defect (getGreeting returns 'hello world' instead of 'hello dolly') and identified the fix site (src/hello.ts). However, the coordinator notes explicitly instructed openspec to 'elaborate and define ACs before implementation,' and openspec did not produce any acceptance criteria (acceptanceChanged: false, splitCount: 0). OpenSpec's note that 'verification criteria are derived from the description alone' is a reasonable practical assessment for a story this trivial, but it does not fulfill the explicit mandate. A human should confirm whether the description-as-spec is sufficient to proceed or whether formalized ACs should be backfilled.",
    "qualityScore": 0.6,
    "flags": ["openspec_did_not_define_acs_as_instructed", "single_agent_run_no_overlap_to_evaluate"]
  }
]
